@MorenoDiDomenico Thanks for opening this issue.

Here is an example that uses the searchFunction and methods to interogate the state of search instead of the DOM:

```js
const search = instantsearch({
  appId: APPID,
  apiKey: APIKEY,
  indexName: 'products',
  urlSync: true,
  searchFunction: function(h) {
    if (search.helper.hasRefinements('cat_color')) {
      search.helper.setQueryParameter('distinct', true);
    } else {
      search.helper.setQueryParameter('distinct', false);
    }

    h.search();
  },
});
```

Thanks for your reply,

I just found a solution (see above) but I suppose your is better.

```
var search = instantsearch({
    appId: APPID,
    apiKey: APPKEY,
    indexName: 'products',
    urlSync: true,
    searchFunction: function(helper){
        if (helper.state.disjunctiveFacetsRefinements.cat_color) {
            search.helper.state.distinct = false;
        } else {
            search.helper.state.distinct = true;
        }
        helper.search();
    }
});
```

Thanks again.

@MorenoDiDomenico helper.setQueryParameter resets the page to 0. In order to prevent you can reassign the current page value or you can do the following:

```js
searchFunction: function(h) {
    if (search.helper.hasRefinements('cat_color')) {
      search.helper.setState(search.helper.state.setQueryParameter('distinct', true));
    } else {
      search.helper.setState(search.helper.state.setQueryParameter('distinct', false));
    }

    h.search();
}
```

You can do this because setState does not impact the pagination.
